output:
{
  "title": "Three open questions in DESIGN.md: settle the core bet, the per-dev cluster, and MCP",
  "purpose": "Record where Priya and Sam each stand on the three items still marked OPEN in docs/DESIGN.md, with reasoning, so the OPEN markers can be resolved into stated decisions (or into a named piece of work that must happen before they can be). Where the two disagree, the disagreement is what gets written down.",
  "resumeHint": "Rewrite the three `OPEN` blocks in docs/DESIGN.md as decisions, quoting each maintainer's reasoning. If item 1 comes back as 'verify first', open that measurement task before touching anything else.",
  "visibility": "open",
  "closeWhen": {
    "kind": "all"
  },
  "participants": [
    {
      "name": "Priya",
      "role": "Maintainer — must sign off before the OPEN markers come out"
    },
    {
      "name": "Sam",
      "role": "Maintainer — must sign off before the OPEN markers come out"
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Where the argument stands",
        "lead": "DESIGN.md commits to one bet and leaves three things unresolved. The bet: the agent-facing surface is a schema an agent emits against, not a framework it programs against, because generating bespoke HTML costs thousands of output tokens every time and that generation — not context loading, not deploy latency — is where the current approach is slow and expensive. A schema emission is claimed at a 20–50× token reduction over an authored page, and that reduction is stated as the entire reason the service exists.",
        "points": [
          "The bet's premise is asserted, not measured. DESIGN.md itself says it is worth confirming against two or three real past generations before committing — and that if context loading turns out to dominate, the right answer is a cached design doc, not this service.",
          "Any design decision that pushes agents back toward writing markup breaks the bet. That is the standing test the other two items get judged against.",
          "The deployment shape is per-dev: each dev runs an independent single-node k3s cluster behind their own tunnel, so a participant link points at that dev's box — a box that is also a NixOS host that gets rebuilt.",
          "The insurance already taken is cheap and unarguable: never bake the host into a brief's identity. A handle plus a participant token identify a brief; the hostname is deployment configuration. That keeps 'move to one shared deployment' a config change rather than a migration.",
          "The REST API is built, bearer-authenticated, and is what the participant-facing app uses. An MCP server over the top — list_widgets, create_brief, await_responses — is proposed but not built. REST stays underneath either way, because an orchestrator resuming a session is not necessarily an agent."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Open item 1 — the core bet rests on an unmeasured premise"
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The two sides of it",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "For proceeding now",
            "detail": "The 20–50× claim is large enough that even a badly wrong estimate leaves the conclusion intact. Schema-over-framework is also defensible on grounds the token argument does not depend on: consistency of output, a reviewable widget vocabulary, and no markup for an agent to get wrong."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "For measuring first",
            "detail": "DESIGN.md names generation as the dominant cost and then names the alternative hypothesis in the same breath: if context loading dominates instead, the correct artefact is a cached design doc and this service is the wrong shape entirely. That is not a rounding error in the estimate — it is a different project. Two or three real past generations would settle it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "The cost of getting it wrong late",
            "detail": "Every decision downstream — the widget vocabulary, the MCP surface, the participant app — is justified by this premise. If it falls, they fall with it, and they fall after they are built."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Item 1 — your position",
        "context": "The measurement DESIGN.md asks for is: take two or three real past generations, and attribute their cost between generation and context loading.",
        "question": "What should happen to the `OPEN` marker on the core bet?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "measure-first",
            "label": "Measure first — block further work on the schema surface until two or three past generations have been attributed"
          },
          {
            "value": "measure-parallel",
            "label": "Measure in parallel — keep building, but treat the bet as provisional and run the attribution this cycle"
          },
          {
            "value": "accept",
            "label": "Accept the bet as stated — remove the OPEN marker, on the strength of the estimate plus the non-token arguments for a schema"
          },
          {
            "value": "reframe",
            "label": "I already believe context loading dominates — reframe toward a cached design doc"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Open item 2 — per-dev clusters and the dead link"
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "The shape of the problem",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "Topology",
            "value": "One independent single-node k3s cluster per dev, each behind that dev's own tunnel"
          },
          {
            "key": "What a link points at",
            "value": "That dev's box, specifically"
          },
          {
            "key": "Failure mode",
            "value": "A stakeholder clicks six hours later and gets an answer only if the box is still up — and the box is a NixOS host that gets rebuilt"
          },
          {
            "key": "Blast radius today",
            "value": "One dev's briefs"
          },
          {
            "key": "Mitigation already taken",
            "value": "Host is never part of a brief's identity — handle plus participant token identify it; hostname is deployment config"
          },
          {
            "key": "Line DESIGN.md draws",
            "value": "Tolerable for a dev-phase tool; not tolerable once links go to people outside the team"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "DESIGN.md's own words: a dead link to an external stakeholder is worse than never having sent one. Nothing currently enforces the internal/external line — it is a convention, not a check."
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Item 2 — your position",
        "context": "The host-independence insurance is already taken and is not in dispute. The question is whether anything more is needed now.",
        "question": "How should the per-dev cluster risk be handled?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "accept-as-is",
            "label": "Accept as is — host-independent identity is sufficient insurance for the dev phase; document the internal-only convention and move on"
          },
          {
            "value": "enforce-internal",
            "label": "Accept, but enforce — keep per-dev clusters and add something that actually prevents or flags sending a link outside the team"
          },
          {
            "value": "shared-now",
            "label": "Move to one shared deployment now, before the convention gets broken by accident"
          },
          {
            "value": "shared-on-trigger",
            "label": "Stay per-dev, but commit to a named trigger that forces the move to shared (first external participant, or a fixed date)"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Open item 3 — MCP server over the REST API"
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "What each option costs and buys",
        "columns": [
          "Option",
          "What it buys",
          "What it costs",
          "Risk to the core bet"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Build MCP now (list_widgets, create_brief, await_responses)",
            "The idiomatic agent-facing surface; no agent has to remember curl invocations",
            "A second surface to keep in step with REST as the widget vocabulary moves",
            "Low — list_widgets pushes agents toward the vocabulary, which is the opposite of writing markup"
          ],
          [
            "Defer until REST usage shows the gap",
            "Evidence about which tools are actually wanted, and what shape they should be",
            "Agents keep hand-rolling curl in the meantime; ergonomics friction goes unmeasured",
            "Low, but the friction may quietly push agents toward doing it themselves"
          ],
          [
            "Skip MCP; REST only",
            "One surface, no drift",
            "Loses the idiomatic surface entirely",
            "Low directly, but a harder-to-use surface is a reason to bypass it"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "REST stays underneath in all three cases — an orchestrator resuming a session is not necessarily an agent, so REST is not an implementation detail of MCP."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Item 3 — your position",
        "context": "The REST API is already built, bearer-authenticated, and serving the participant-facing app. This is purely about what sits on top.",
        "question": "Should the MCP server be built, and when?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "build-now",
            "label": "Build it now, with the three tools as proposed"
          },
          {
            "value": "build-now-smaller",
            "label": "Build it now, but with a different or smaller tool set — say which in your reasoning"
          },
          {
            "value": "defer",
            "label": "Defer — leave the OPEN marker, revisit once there is real REST usage to learn from"
          },
          {
            "value": "skip",
            "label": "Don't build it — REST only"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "widget": "rate-items",
      "props": {
        "title": "How settled does each item feel to you?",
        "items": [
          {
            "id": "confidence-core-bet",
            "label": "The core bet (schema, not framework) — 1 means it needs evidence before anyone builds on it, 5 means it is settled"
          },
          {
            "id": "confidence-cluster",
            "label": "Per-dev clusters and link durability — 1 means this bites us soon, 5 means the insurance already taken is enough"
          },
          {
            "id": "confidence-mcp",
            "label": "The MCP surface — 1 means we are guessing, 5 means the shape is obvious"
          }
        ],
        "scale": 5
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "ask",
      "props": {
        "title": "Anything the write-up gets wrong",
        "context": "The three items above are as DESIGN.md frames them. If the framing itself is off — a fourth thing that should be open, or an item that is not really contested — say so here.",
        "field": {
          "kind": "textarea",
          "id": "framing-objections",
          "label": "Corrections to the framing, or a fourth open item",
          "required": false
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
